  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the first run, which used one of the six runs: 9 witnesses passed and the rule composes no other rule.\n\n**What the rule says**\n- **Who it covers:** any vehicle, as the admitted aggregate `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`.\n- **When it applies:** the vehicle is on a highway, the place is not declared to be in a business or residence district, and the vehicle is not disabled. \"Disabled\" is read as engine off, hazard lamps on, and standing on the roadway.\n- **What it forbids:** a vehicle halted on the roadway while a shoulder lies within `p.offroad_stop_reach` of its body.\n- **When a halt is still allowed:** the vehicle is close behind another road user ahead in its lane (within `p.stopping_conflict_gap`), or it is stopped where one of these controls it:\n  - a police officer directing stop;\n  - a signal showing circular red, a red arrow or flashing red;\n  - a stop sign.\n\n**Choices I made where the node was open** (none needed a declared narrowing):\n- The \"on the roadway\" condition sits in the duty itself rather than in when the rule applies, because that is where the node's obligation text puts it.\n- \"A red arrow\" covers all four arrow directions.\n- The gap to the vehicle ahead is read as a straight distance.\n\n**Witnesses:** the six planned scenes are built as narrated. The `permit` scene puts the nearest shoulder about 58 m away, beyond the end of the bridge. I added three scenes, `branch_3` to `branch_5`: the `forbid` scene plus a police officer, a red signal or a stop sign, each expecting compliant. Without them the device exceptions would not be tested.\n\nEarlier I mistakenly ran the gate command with `--help` added. The sandbox blocked it and it did not run, so it was not a gate run.\n\nFiles are in the working directory:\n- task_result.md",
